1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,310
In this simulated town, on average, three quarters of a household's neighbors match its color.

2
00:00:06,610 --> 00:00:09,180
How much matching do these households demand?

3
00:00:11,133 --> 00:00:12,263
Just a third.

4
00:00:12,513 --> 00:00:16,093
Each household counts the occupied squares around it.

5
00:00:16,393 --> 00:00:22,003
If fewer than a third match, it moves, so it is happy to be outnumbered two to one.

6
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:27,160
We read real city maps the same way, guessing motives from patterns.

7
00:00:27,660 --> 00:00:32,000
So how can a demand of one third build a town of three quarters?

8
00:00:32,666 --> 00:00:35,106
Rewind to the start, a random mix.

9
00:00:35,606 --> 00:00:39,536
Here, on average, half the neighbors match, well above a third.

10
00:00:39,800 --> 00:00:42,720
Still, these two hundred thirty-eight are unhappy.

11
00:00:43,166 --> 00:00:48,856
The obvious plan is to move each one to a random empty square where it would be content.

12
00:00:49,056 --> 00:00:52,576
Then everyone is content, and the town stays mixed.

13
00:00:53,033 --> 00:00:58,343
Round one takes two hundred moves; the other thirty-eight were content by their turn.

14
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:01,910
If the plan had worked, this counter would read zero.

15
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:04,020
It reads seventy-four.

16
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:08,280
Sixty-seven of these were content before round one.

17
00:01:08,430 --> 00:01:09,630
They never moved.

18
00:01:10,030 --> 00:01:11,260
Their neighbors did.

19
00:01:11,866 --> 00:01:13,226
Replay one corner.

20
00:01:13,526 --> 00:01:17,966
At the start, this gold household had three gold neighbors out of eight.

21
00:01:18,466 --> 00:01:21,176
Three eighths beats a third, so it stayed.

22
00:01:21,633 --> 00:01:23,343
Then this neighbor moved away.

23
00:01:23,643 --> 00:01:25,103
Is it still content?

24
00:01:26,766 --> 00:01:28,446
Two gold out of seven.

25
00:01:28,946 --> 00:01:32,706
Two sevenths is less than a third, so now it wants out.

26
00:01:33,166 --> 00:01:35,626
Its neighbor here also has three eighths.

27
00:01:35,926 --> 00:01:40,506
In round two, our household leaves, and this one drops to two sevenths.

28
00:01:41,100 --> 00:01:42,570
Now run the whole town.

29
00:01:43,970 --> 00:01:46,660
After round seven, nobody wants to move.

30
00:01:48,366 --> 00:01:50,986
That whole town is one point on this chart.

31
00:01:52,200 --> 00:01:57,430
Demand a quarter, and the same starting town barely sorts: fifty-eight percent.

32
00:01:57,930 --> 00:02:00,990
Demand a third, and it jumps to seventy-six.

33
00:02:02,033 --> 00:02:07,753
To see why that happens, sort the random start by each household's share of matching neighbors.

34
00:02:08,053 --> 00:02:12,723
With at most eight neighbors, these are the only shares up to sixty percent.

35
00:02:13,333 --> 00:02:20,873
At a third, everyone left of it is unhappy, including the neighbor that started our corner's chain, at two sevenths.

36
00:02:21,333 --> 00:02:25,383
Every household at three eighths is one lost match away from it.

37
00:02:25,833 --> 00:02:28,483
At a quarter, how many would start unhappy?

38
00:02:30,233 --> 00:02:37,823
Only these two bars change sides, and they're tall: just ninety-five start unhappy, and that chain never starts.

39
00:02:39,166 --> 00:02:43,286
In his 1971 paper, Thomas Schelling ran this model.

40
00:02:43,586 --> 00:02:52,796
His households moved to the nearest suitable square, on emptier boards, and there the jump came later, between about a third and a half.

41
00:02:54,100 --> 00:02:57,680
Two simulated towns, both seventy-six percent.

42
00:02:58,180 --> 00:03:01,000
In one town, households demand a third.

43
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:04,330
In the other, nobody minds their neighbors at all.

44
00:03:04,830 --> 00:03:06,460
Which one wanted a third?

45
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:09,240
This one.

46
00:03:09,440 --> 00:03:12,770
The other was placed by a rule, with no preferences.

47
00:03:13,070 --> 00:03:23,160
In real cities, one study finds that 1930s federal lending maps increased racial segregation for decades, through reduced credit.

48
00:03:23,966 --> 00:03:25,716
Look again at the first town.

49
00:03:26,016 --> 00:03:32,236
As Schelling argued, whatever you guessed its people demanded, the map alone could not have told you.

